<p>
Marcin Borkowski (mbork) has an interesting post on <a href="https://mbork.pl/2026-10-26_Unicode_punctuation_in_Emacs">Unicode punctuation in Emacs</a>. His post is mainly concerned with the vagaries of dealing with English and Polish punctuation in the same document but a lot of the heavy lifting is accomplished with <a href="https://www.gnu.org/software/emacs/manual/html_node/emacs/Quotation-Marks.html">Electric Quote Mode</a>.
</p>
<p>
The main problem is handling single and double quotes ‘like this’ and “like this”. Because I do almost all my writing in Org mode, I use the <code>':t</code> option to turn the ASCII double quote (<code>"</code>) into their curved typeset equivalents on export. That’s supposed to do the same with single quotes but I could never get it to work for me and always had to add then manually.
</p>
<p>
Fortunately, it’s easy to make everything work right in the buffer as you type. You merely turn <code>electric-quote-mode</code> on and it takes care of everything for you. By default it acts the way TeX used to by requiring &#8220; for an opening double quote and &#8221; for the closing double quote and similarly for single quotes. You can make it accept &#8221; and &#8216; instead by setting <code>electric-quote-replace-double</code> and <code>electric-quote-context-sensitive</code> to <code>t</code>.
</p>
<p>
Electric Quote Mode is global so turning it on enables it for all your buffers. You can use <code>electric-quote-local-mode</code> if you want it only in the current buffer. You can toggle either of them on or off by simply calling <kbd>Meta</kbd>+<kbd>x</kbd> <code>electric-quote...</code>.
</p>
<p>
If you have to deal different quote notation for two or more languages, take a look at mbork’s post for a nifty solution.</p>
